nse that we're not going to have these  very nice structured titles to just  piece together  we're interested in both an attractive  summarization that makes sense to users  on its own but also that you'll have to  piece together from  full sentences  right so once again to give you a sense  of what the  data set we ended up compiling looked  like  so the tables themselves would be quite  more complicated than wikipedia ones  which were the usual use case in the  google paper  and you would have some kind of a label  uh given by the actual authors of the  paper which would be the the  title or the caption that they gave for  the table depends on the format of the  paper  um sometimes it would be quite long  which is another challenge  um and you would usually also have  a linking text or a linking paragraph as  we called it sometimes it would be one  paragraph sometimes it would be longer  it really depends but the idea was that  we wanted a mechanism to  first of all find the references to the  table name  across the document and then use those  as a specific  training data we're interested in for  each table such that we don't have to  analyze  the full uh document even though we  experimented with different settings of  that as well  obviously um you can think of it as like  another  hyperparameter choice of like how much  of the text you want your  model to see and to rely on and we also  really were interested in  mixtures of of topics  of domain expertise and we also noticed  that a lot of  what were perceived to be good titles by  let's say at least the team we were  working with at the time  uh seemed to follow these very specific  patterns  so you could almost be tempted and i  think some of the people that i worked  with were actually tempted  to take a very hands-on approach and  just say  how about we just do something very  similar to regular expression or we  start with some kind of a  pattern matching particular people who  came from more  traditional